(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-08"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A function f from a set A to a set B gives each input in A exactly one output in B. The set A of inputs is the domain, which is the set of x-values for which f(x) is defined. B is the co-domain. The range is the set of outputs that really occur, and it can be smaller than B. On a graph, the domain lies along the x-axis and the range along the y-axis.","w":["Each input must have exactly one output: a vertical line cuts the graph of a function at most once.","Range and co-domain are different: the range is only the values actually reached."],"x":["Think of a function f as a rule that takes each element of a set A and gives back exactly one element of a set B. The set A of the inputs is the domain. The set B is the co-domain. The range is the set of the outputs that really occur, and it can be smaller than B. We write y = f(x), where x is the input and y is the output.","The domain of a function given by a formula is the largest set of numbers for which the formula gives a real value. Look for the dangers: a denominator that is zero, a square root of a negative number, and the logarithm of a number that is zero or negative. For f(x) = √(x − 2), the domain is x ≥ 2. For f(x) = 1/(x² − 9), it is all real numbers except ±3. For f(x) = log(x − 1), it is x greater than 1.","To find the range, solve y = f(x) for x, and see which values of y give a real x. For a quadratic, complete the square. For f(x) = x² − 4x + 7 = (x − 2)² + 3, the range is y ≥ 3, that is [3, ∞).","Common mistakes: using the words range and co-domain as if they meant the same, and giving the same input two outputs. A relation like x = y² is not a function, since x = 4 gives y = 2 and y = −2."],"e":"f(x) = √(x − 2)/(x − 5). The square root needs x ≥ 2, and the denominator needs x ≠ 5. So the domain is [2, 5) together with (5, ∞)."},"6.2":{"s":"A function is one-one if different inputs always give different outputs. It is onto if every element of the co-domain is an output of some input. A function that is both is a bijection, and only a bijection has an inverse. If f takes A to B and g takes B to C, the composite function g∘f applies f first and then g. The inverse of f takes each output back to its input.","f":[{"x":"f(x_1) = f(x_2) => x_1 = x_2","n":"Test for a one-one function f. For onto: every y in the co-domain equals f(x) for some x in the domain.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.128"},{"x":"(g∘f)(x) = g(f(x))","n":"Composite function: first f, then g.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.128"}],"w":["In g∘f the function nearest to x acts first: f first, then g. And g∘f is usually not the same as f∘g.","A function has an inverse only if it is both one-one and onto."],"x":["A function is one-one (injective) if different inputs always give different outputs: f(x₁) = f(x₂) implies x₁ = x₂. On a graph, no horizontal line meets the curve more than once. A function is onto (surjective) if every element of the co-domain is the output of at least one input, so the range is the whole co-domain. A function that is both one-one and onto is a bijection.","Counting: the number of functions from a set of m elements to a set of n elements is nᵐ. The number of one-one functions (m is at most n) is nPm = n(n − 1)…(n − m + 1), so from 3 to 5 it is 60. The number of onto functions from 4 elements to 2 elements is 2⁴ − 2 = 14.","The composite function: if f takes A to B and g takes B to C, then (g∘f)(x) = g(f(x)). The function nearest to x acts first. In general, g∘f is not the same as f∘g. For f(x) = x + 1 and g(x) = x², (g∘f)(2) = 9, but (f∘g)(2) = 5.","The inverse function f⁻¹ takes each output back to its input. It exists only if f is a bijection. To find it, write y = f(x), solve for x in terms of y, and swap the names. For f(x) = 3x − 2, f⁻¹(x) = (x + 2)/3. Common mistake: writing f⁻¹(x) as 1/f(x). They are different."],"e":"f(x) = 3x − 2 is one-one and onto the real numbers, so it has an inverse, f⁻¹(x) = (x + 2)/3. Then f⁻¹(7) = 3, and f(3) = 7. The function g(x) = x² is not one-one on the reals, since g(2) = g(−2), so it has no inverse there."},"6.3":{"s":"A function can be shown in several ways: as a formula, as a table of inputs and outputs, as a set of ordered pairs, as an arrow diagram between two sets, or as a graph in the plane. All of these carry the same information. A graph is the most useful way to see the domain and range, the increasing and decreasing parts, and whether the function is one-one.","w":["A graph is a function only if no vertical line meets it twice, and it is one-one if no horizontal line meets it twice."],"x":["A function can be shown in several ways, and all of them carry the same information. A formula, such as f(x) = 2x + 1. A table of the inputs and the outputs. A set of ordered pairs, such as {(1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7)}. An arrow diagram that joins each element of A to its image in B. And a graph in the plane, in which the point (x, f(x)) is plotted for each x of the domain.","The graph is the most useful way to see the properties of a function. The domain is the set of x-values along the horizontal axis, and the range is the set of y-values along the vertical axis. You can see where the function is increasing or decreasing, and where it is zero.","Two tests with lines. The vertical line test: a curve is the graph of a function only if no vertical line meets it at more than one point, since each x must have one y. The horizontal line test: a function is one-one if no horizontal line meets its graph at more than one point.","Common mistake: thinking that every curve is the graph of a function. A circle is not, since the vertical line through its middle meets it at two points."],"e":"The graph of y = x² passes the vertical line test, so it is a function. But it fails the horizontal line test (the line y = 4 meets it at x = 2 and x = −2), so it is not one-one on the real numbers."},"6.4":{"s":"Some functions are used again and again. A constant function gives one fixed value. The identity function returns its input. A polynomial function is built from whole powers of x; the linear, quadratic and cubic functions are the common ones. The square root function is defined only for non-negative x. A rational function is a ratio of polynomials and excludes the zeros of the denominator. The exponential and logarithmic functions have fixed domains and ranges.","f":[{"x":"f(x) = sqrt(x)","n":"Domain [0, ∞), range [0, ∞).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = p(x)/q(x)","n":"Rational function: domain is all real x except where q(x) = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = a^x, a > 1","n":"Exponential function: domain R, range (0, ∞).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = log_a x, a > 1","n":"Logarithmic function: domain (0, ∞), range R.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"}],"w":["The domain of a square root or a logarithm excludes the values that make the inside negative (or zero, for a log).","The exponential never reaches 0 or a negative value, so its range is (0, ∞), not R."],"x":["Some functions are used again and again. A constant function, f(x) = c, gives the same output for every input. Its graph is a horizontal line. The identity function, f(x) = x, returns its input. A polynomial function is made of whole powers of x: linear (ax + b), quadratic (ax² + bx + c), cubic, and so on. The domain of a polynomial is all real numbers.","A rational function is a ratio of two polynomials, p(x)/q(x). Its domain excludes the zeros of the denominator. The square root function, f(x) = √x, is defined only for x ≥ 0, and its range is also y ≥ 0. The exponential function, eˣ (or aˣ), has all real numbers as its domain and the positive numbers as its range, (0, ∞). It never reaches 0 or a negative value. The logarithmic function, log x, is its inverse. It is defined for positive x, and its range is all real numbers.","To find the domain of a combination, use all the restrictions together. For √(x − 2)/(x − 5), use x ≥ 2 and x ≠ 5. For log(x − 1), use x greater than 1.","Common mistakes: including the value where the logarithm is zero (the inside must be positive, not just non-negative), and giving all real numbers as the range of eˣ."],"e":"Domains: f(x) = x² + 1 is all real numbers. f(x) = 1/(x − 4) is all real numbers except 4. f(x) = √(5 − x) is x ≤ 5. f(x) = ln(x + 3) is x greater than −3. The range of eˣ is (0, ∞)."},"6.5":{"s":"A piece-wise function uses different rules on different parts of its domain, so each input must be put into the rule for its own part. Special functions of this kind are the absolute value or modulus, which is never negative, the greatest integer function that gives the whole number just below or at x, the fractional part function, and the signum function that returns −1, 0 or 1 according to the sign of x.","f":[{"x":"f(x) = |x|","n":"Modulus (absolute) function: domain R, range [0, ∞).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = sgn(x)","n":"Signum function: domain R, range {-1, 0, 1}.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"}],"w":["Check which part of the domain the input belongs to before using a rule; at the break point use the rule that includes it.","[x] of a negative number goes down: [−2.3] = −3, not −2."],"x":["A piece-wise function uses different rules on different parts of its domain. To find a value, first see which part the input belongs to, and then use the rule of that part. At the break point, use the rule that includes the break point (a ≥ or ≤ sign).","The absolute value (modulus) function is |x| = x for x at least 0, and −x for x below 0. It is never negative, and it gives the distance of x from zero. The signum function is sgn(x) = 1 for positive x, 0 for x = 0, and −1 for negative x. The greatest integer function [x] is the greatest integer that is less than or equal to x. So [3.7] = 3, [5] = 5, and [−2.3] = −3, since for a negative number it goes down. The fractional part is {x} = x − [x], which is always from 0 up to, but not including, 1.","To evaluate, work step by step. For f(x) = |x − 3| + sgn(x − 1) at x = −2: |−5| = 5, and sgn(−3) = −1, so f(−2) = 4.","Common mistakes: using [x] = −2 for x = −2.3 (the answer is −3), and using the wrong part of a piece-wise rule at the break point."],"e":"f(x) = x² for negative x, and 2x + 1 for x at least 0. Then f(−3) = 9, and f(2) = 5. At the break point x = 0, the second rule applies, so f(0) = 1."}};
